# XSH fgetpos()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.fgetpos.001
anchor     functions/fgetpos.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fgetpos.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fgetpos() returns 0 and stores a value fsetpos() can return to: on
           a stream reading "0123456789", a value stored after 3 reads takes
           the stream back to '3' after 4 more reads; on a "w" stream, a
           value stored after fputs("hello") (still buffered) takes it back
           to offset 5 after fputs("XY"), so that fputc('Z') and fclose()
           leave "helloZY".
note       fgetpos() came from ISO C (Issue 4); 2001 is the first POSIX.1
           edition with it. What the value holds is unspecified and is not
           looked at (variant fgetpos.opaque).
test       t/001-round-trip.c

assertion  XSH.fgetpos.002
anchor     functions/fgetpos.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With errno set to ERANGE, a successful fgetpos() on a file stream
           leaves errno ERANGE.
test       t/002-errno-kept.c

assertion  XSH.fgetpos.003
anchor     functions/fgetpos.html#ERRORS/ESPIPE
class      required
since      2001
says       fgetpos() on streams from fdopen() of a pipe's read end, of a FIFO
           opened O_RDONLY|O_NONBLOCK, and of one end of an AF_UNIX
           socketpair() returns a non-zero value with errno ESPIPE.
note       Issue 6 added the error, sockets included.
test       t/003-espipe.c

assertion  XSH.fgetpos.004
anchor     functions/fgetpos.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      required
since      2001
says       On a stream reading "0123456789" whose descriptor has been closed
           with close() after one read and a successful fgetpos(), fgetpos()
           returns a non-zero value with errno EBADF.
note       Issue 6 added the error. close() does not move the offset, so
           the descriptor is not a second handle (XSH 2.5.1).
test       t/004-ebadf.c
